@page "/stream"
@inject JobRepository Repository

@code {
    [CascadingParameter] public HttpContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private JobFilter filter = new();
    private StreamResult chunk = null!;
    private int limit = 25;
    private int cancelAfter = 10;

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        var query = Context.Request.Query;

        if (int.TryParse(query["limit"], out var requested)) limit = Math.Clamp(requested, 1, 200);
        if (int.TryParse(query["cancel_after"], out var after)) cancelAfter = Math.Clamp(after, 0, 200);

        filter = JobFilter.FromQuery(query);

        chunk = await Repository.GetChunkAsync(filter, after: 0, limit, cancelAfter > 0 ? cancelAfter : null,
            Context.RequestAborted);
    }
}

<PageTitle>Streaming</PageTitle>

<h2>Streaming</h2>

<p class="lede">
    <code>EnumerateToAsync</code> yields one record at a time, so a result set that does not fit in
    memory can still be read, and the token is checked between records. htmx is what shows that
    incrementally: each chunk asks for the next one when it is revealed, and the chain stops when a
    chunk comes back with no more rows.
</p>

<p class="note">
    Each chunk is its own request, so the cursor is a <code>job_id</code> rather than a live reader.
    Nothing can survive between requests, which is the honest limit of doing this without a circuit.
    Without htmx this page shows the first chunk only.
</p>

<p class="note">
    The Run button swaps the chunk region instead of navigating, which is what keeps the page where you
    were: a swap does not move the scroll position, a document navigation does. Without htmx the form
    submits to <code>/stream</code> as a normal GET and the browser resets scroll, as it does on every
    full navigation. A swap leaves the address bar alone, so the endpoint answers with an
    <code>HX-Push-Url</code> header holding the page URL, which is what a refresh or a shared link
    would load. Only the run itself is pushed: the chunks the chain appends would each overwrite the
    address bar with a cursor.
</p>

<form method="get" action="/stream" hx-get="/stream/chunk" hx-target="#chunk" hx-swap="outerHTML" hx-push-url="true" class="filter fields">
    <label>
        Rows per chunk
        <input type="number" name="limit" value="@limit" min="1" max="200" />
    </label>

    <label>
        Cancel after
        <input type="number" name="cancel_after" value="@cancelAfter" min="0" max="200" />
    </label>

    <label>
        Customer contains
        <input type="text" name="customer_like" value="@filter.CustomerLike" />
    </label>

    <div class="field">
        <span>&nbsp;</span>
        <button type="submit">Run</button>
    </div>
</form>

<CodeSnippet Code="@snippet" />

<StreamChunk Chunk="@chunk" FilterQuery="@filter.ToFilterQueryString()" />

@code {
    private const string snippet = """
        await foreach (var job in reader.EnumerateToAsync<Job>(options, cancellationToken))
            Handle(job);
        """;
}
